{"step_index":66,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T14:08:26Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-22T18:08:26+04:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-22T18:08:26+04:00\nFile Path: `file:///home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/4cb12149-1140-450b-b195-316320800a2b/.system_generated/steps/64/content.md`\nTotal Lines: 144\nTotal Bytes: 10592\nShowing lines 1 to 144\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: Title: Live Content\n2: \n3: Description: Fetched live\n4: \n5: Source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/yctimlin/mcp_excalidraw/main/README.md\n6: \n7: ---\n8: \n9: # Excalidraw MCP Server, CLI & Agent Skill\n10: \n11: [![CI](https://github.com/yctimlin/mcp_excalidraw/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/yctimlin/mcp_excalidraw/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n12: [![Docker Build & Push](https://github.com/yctimlin/mcp_excalidraw/actions/workflows/docker.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/yctimlin/mcp_excalidraw/actions/workflows/docker.yml)\n13: [![NPM Version](https://img.shields.io/npm/v/mcp-excalidraw-server)](https://www.npmjs.com/package/mcp-excalidraw-server)\n14: [![License](https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-blue.svg)](LICENSE)\n15: \n16: **mcp-excalidraw-server** gives AI agents a live [Excalidraw](https://excalidraw.com) canvas they can draw on, look at, refine, and save into your repo. Your agent creates architecture diagrams and flowcharts programmatically, **sees its own work via screenshots**, fixes layout problems, and exports `.excalidraw` files you can commit next to your code.\n17: \n18: One canvas, three ways to drive it:\n19: \n20: - **Agent Skill + CLI** — recommended for coding agents (Claude Code, Codex CLI, Cursor, OpenCode): `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server <command>`. Zero config, auto-starts the canvas, composable JSON in/out.\n21: - **MCP Server** — 26 tools over stdio for any Model Context Protocol client (Claude Desktop, Cursor, Codex CLI, Antigravity, ...). Speaks MCP `2026-07-28` (`server/discover`, per-request `_meta` envelope, tool calls without a handshake) and stays compatible with 2025-era clients that open with `initialize`.\n22: - **REST API** — plain HTTP for LangChain and custom frameworks.\n23: \n24: Core drawing runs fully local (Node ≥ 20, MIT licensed) — no API keys. Mermaid conversion runs in the local browser canvas; `share` is optional and uploads an encrypted scene to excalidraw.com.\n25: \n26: ## Demo\n27: \n28: ![AI agent drawing an architecture diagram on a live Excalidraw canvas via MCP](demo.gif)\n29: \n30: *AI agent creates a complete architecture diagram from a single prompt (4x speed). [Watch full video on YouTube](https://youtu.be/ufW78Amq5qA)*\n31: \n32: ## Table of Contents\n33: \n34: - [Demo](#demo)\n35: - [What It Is](#what-it-is)\n36: - [How We Differ from the Official Excalidraw MCP](#how-we-differ-from-the-official-excalidraw-mcp)\n37: - [What's New](#whats-new)\n38: - [Installation](#installation)\n39: - [Agent Skill](#agent-skill)\n40: - [CLI Reference](#cli-reference)\n41: - [Configure MCP Clients](#configure-mcp-clients)\n42:   - [Claude Desktop](#claude-desktop)\n43:   - [Claude Code](#claude-code)\n44:   - [Cursor](#cursor)\n45:   - [Codex CLI](#codex-cli)\n46:   - [OpenCode](#opencode)\n47:   - [Antigravity (Google)](#antigravity-google)\n48: - [MCP Tools (26 Total)](#mcp-tools-26-total)\n49: - [Quick Start (From Source / Docker)](#quick-start-from-source--docker)\n50: - [Testing](#testing)\n51: - [FAQ](#faq)\n52: - [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting)\n53: - [Known Issues / TODO](#known-issues--todo)\n54: - [Development](#development)\n55: - [License](#license)\n56: \n57: ## What It Is\n58: \n59: Ask your agent to *\"draw the architecture of this service\"* and it produces a real, editable Excalidraw diagram — not a one-shot image. Because the agent can query, screenshot, and update individual elements, it iterates until labels fit, nothing overlaps, and arrows route cleanly; then it exports the result as a `.excalidraw` file that lives in your repo and gets updated when the code changes.\n60: \n61: Under the hood there are two processes, one product:\n62: \n63: - **Canvas server**: Excalidraw web UI + REST API + WebSocket real-time sync (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`)\n64: - **A thin front-end of your choice**: the CLI, the MCP stdio server, or raw HTTP — all drive the same canvas\n65: \n66: Since v1.1 the canvas server starts itself: canvas-driving CLI commands (and the MCP server on launch) auto-spawn it if nothing is listening. `status` only inspects the current server state. Set `EXCALIDRAW_NO_AUTOSTART=1` to opt out.\n67: \n68: ## How We Differ from the Official Excalidraw MCP\n69: \n70: Excalidraw has an [official MCP](https://github.com/excalidraw/excalidraw-mcp) — a chat widget that streams a diagram inline from a single prompt (the model gets two tools: a format reference and `create_view`). It's great for \"draw me a cat\" in Claude or ChatGPT. We solve a different problem: giving *coding agents* a persistent canvas workbench.\n71: \n72: | | Official Excalidraw MCP | This Project |\n73: |---|---|---|\n74: | **Approach** | Prompt in, diagram out (one-shot widget) | Programmatic element-level control (CLI + 26 MCP tools) |\n75: | **State** | Checkpoints inside the chat widget | Persistent live canvas with real-time sync |\n76: | **Element CRUD** | Declarative re-send with delete markers | Full create / read / update / delete per element |\n77: | **AI sees the canvas** | No | `describe` (structured text) + `screenshot` (image) |\n78: | **Iterative refinement** | Regenerate from checkpoint | Draw → look → adjust → look again, element by element |\n79: | **Layout tools** | No | align, distribute, group / ungroup, lock, duplicate |\n80: | **File I/O** | No model-facing export | `.excalidraw` export/import — diagrams as repo artifacts |\n81: | **Snapshot & rollback** | Widget-side checkpoints | Named server-side snapshots |\n82: | **Mermaid conversion** | No | `mermaid` / `create_from_mermaid` |\n83: | **Shareable URLs** | Widget-only | `share` / `export_to_excalidraw_url` |\n84: | **Viewport control** | Camera animations | `set_viewport` (zoom-to-fit all or selected elements, center on one element, manual zoom) |\n85: | **Works without MCP** | No | Yes — CLI + agent skill + REST API |\n86: | **Multi-agent** | Single chat | Multiple agents on the same canvas concurrently |\n87: \n88: **TL;DR** — The official MCP shows Excalidraw diagrams in your chat. This project gives your coding agent a full Excalidraw workbench: a canvas it can draw on, inspect, refine, and commit to your repo.\n89: \n90: ## What's New\n91: \n92: Current package version: **2.0.0**. The current release line is **v2.0 — Interchange-Grade Exports & MCP 2026-07-28**.\n93: \n94: ### v2.0 — Interchange-Grade Exports & MCP 2026-07-28\n95: \n96: - **Breaking: Node >= 20 required** (was 18) — the MCP TypeScript SDK v2 sets the floor. Everything else is backward compatible, including existing MCP client configs.\n97: - **MCP protocol revision 2026-07-28**: modern clients can call tools statelessly without an initialization handshake (`server/discover`, per-request `_meta` envelopes); legacy initialization-based clients keep working unchanged. (#98, thanks @anxkhn)\n98: - **Exports render everywhere now**: `.excalidraw` / `.excalidraw.md` files contain real Excalidraw elements — shape and arrow labels as bound text, live arrow bindings — so they open correctly on excalidraw.com and in the Obsidian Excalidraw plugin instead of losing labels (or being re-saved empty by the plugin). (#93, #95)\n99: - **Byte-stable exports**: deterministic ids, seeds, and key order — re-exporting an unchanged scene is byte-identical, so committed diagrams and vault files never produce phantom git diffs, and Obsidian block references survive re-exports.\n100: - **Obsidian vault fixes**: Windows/CRLF `.excalidraw.md` files import correctly (#94, thanks @cason-miles); `## Text Elements` block references now cover shape labels too.\n101: - **Element fields are never silently dropped**: unknown Excalidraw properties (`containerId`, `textAlign`, `originalText`, ...) pass through the server intact — fixes browser-edited text vanishing after sync. (#92, thanks @junuxyz)\n102: - **Mermaid conversion merges** into the existing canvas instead of replacing it, and each browser tab holds exactly one WebSocket connection (no more doubled labels). (#91)\n103: - **Viewport control**: `set_viewport` gains `scrollToElementIds` (multi-element zoom-to-fit) and `viewportZoomFactor`, with strict single-mode validation and real error reporting. (#86, thanks @acercyc)\n104: - **Dark mode**: the canvas page chrome follows the editor theme and persists it across reloads. (#89, thanks @danielsvane)\n105: \n106: ### v1.1 — CLI-First\n107: \n108: - **First-class CLI**: every capability is now a composable command — `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server add|query|describe|screenshot|export|import|mermaid|snapshot|arrange|share|...` — JSON on stdout, meaningful exit codes. Also installed as the `excalidraw-canvas` alias.\n109: - **Zero-setup**: canvas-driving CLI commands and the MCP server **auto-start the canvas server** if it isn't running (closes #66). Opt out with `EXCALIDRAW_NO_AUTOSTART=1`.\n110: - **`apply`**: multi-op patches (`{\"create\":[...],\"update\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"set\":{...}}],\"delete\":[...]}`) in a single invocation.\n111: - **`install-skill`**: `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server install-skill --dir <skills-root>` copies the portable agent skill into the directory your agent chooses (project or global), cleanly replacing older versions.\n112: - **Skill is now CLI-first** and no longer needs a cloned repo or configured MCP server to work.\n113: - **Typed queries**: `query --filter locked=true --filter label.text=API` — booleans, numbers, and nested keys work.\n114: - **Internals**: shared core library (`src/core/`) behind both the CLI and MCP server; canvas `groupIds` are the source of truth for grouping (ungroup now works across restarts); `node-fetch` dropped; MCP version metadata derived from `package.json`; canvas server writes a pidfile and shuts down cleanly.\n115: \n116: ## Installation\n117: \n118: The only prerequisite is **Node.js ≥ 20**.\n119: \n120: ### Easiest: let your agent install it\n121: \n122: Copy this into your coding agent — it installs the portable skill into the project/global skill directory that agent already knows how to use, then verifies it by drawing a test diagram:\n123: \n124: ```text\n125: Install the Excalidraw canvas toolkit so you can draw diagrams for me:\n126: \n127: 1. Choose the right skill directory for this agent and scope (project or global).\n128: 2. Run: npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server install-skill --dir <that-skills-directory>\n129: 3. Read the installed excalidraw-skill/SKILL.md so you know the drawing workflow.\n130: 4. Start the canvas with: npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server start\n131:    then tell me to open http://127.0.0.1:3000 in my browser (screenshots need an open tab).\n132: 5. Draw a small test diagram — two labeled boxes connected by an arrow — take a\n133:    screenshot, and show me the result to confirm everything works.\n134: ```\n135: \n136: ### Manual install\n137: \n138: | You are... | Install with | Then |\n139: |---|---|---|\n140: | **Modern coding agent** | `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server install-skill --dir <skills-root>` | Let the agent choose project/global scope and its skill root |\n141: | **Claude Code shortcut** | `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server install-skill` | Installs to `~/.claude/skills` for backward compatibility |\n142: | **Codex shortcut** | `npx -y mcp-excalidraw-server install-skill --ta\n143: \n144: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
